summary:
Since 2000, NASA satellites have recorded every active fire on Earth, but the record is split between two camera generations. The newer VIIRS camera counts about 4 times more fire spots than the older MODIS cameras for the same fires, so every new satellite makes fire look as if it suddenly doubled.

Atlas on Fire fixes this. A machine-learning model (LightGBM), chosen in a contest of six methods scored on years it never saw, translates 26 years of MODIS detections onto the VIIRS scale. The result is one fair record of 584 million detections, from November 2000 to today, for the whole planet.

On top of that record we built a free public web app:

a 3D night globe of fire, month by month;
a burning-activity calendar and a tested early warning for any place;
a live agent that checks NASA's near-real-time feed every 3 hours and flags unusual fires;
an AI assistant whose every number is checked against the data.
It matters because responders, land managers and scientists can finally ask "is this fire season unusual?" and get an answer that is not distorted by a change of satellite.
